# The Maschine MK2's HID interface, and ONLY that one.
#
# NARROWED 2026-09-04, and the measurement is the point. The rule used to match
# on idVendor/idProduct alone, so it claimed every hidraw node this device
# presents and restarted the service on each `add`. Measured on the rig:
#
#   1-1.4:1.0  class 01  snd-usb-audio   (audio)
#   1-1.4:1.1  class 01  snd-usb-audio   (audio)
#   1-1.4:1.2  class 03  usbhid          -> hidraw1   <- the only HID interface
#   1-1.4:1.3  class fe  driver          (application specific)
#
# So the device presents exactly ONE hidraw node and the old rule's hazard was
# theoretical for a single controller - but it is real for a second MK2, which
# would take the symlink and restart the daemon out from under the first.
#
# WHY `KERNELS` AND NOT `ATTRS{bInterfaceNumber}`. Every parent-matching key in
# ONE udev rule must resolve on the SAME parent device, and these two do not:
# `bInterfaceNumber` lives on the interface (1-1.4:1.2) while `idVendor` and
# `idProduct` live on the USB device above it (1-1.4). Tested on the rig with
# `udevadm test` - the combined rule matches NOTHING, silently, which would
# have left /dev/maschine missing and the instrument dead with no error.
#
# `KERNELS=="0003:17CC:1140.*"` matches the HID device that is hidraw's direct
# parent. It encodes the bus (0003 = USB), the vendor and the product in one
# key on one parent, so it needs no ATTRS at all - and it cannot match the
# touchscreen's hidraw0, which was verified the same way.
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", KERNELS=="0003:17CC:1140.*", MODE="0664", GROUP="audio", SYMLINK+="maschine"
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", KERNELS=="0003:17CC:1140.*", ACTION=="add", RUN+="/bin/systemctl --no-block restart maschine-mk2.service"
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", KERNELS=="0003:17CC:1140.*", ACTION=="remove", RUN+="/bin/systemctl --no-block stop maschine-mk2.service"
